        "Attributes": [
          {
            "Id": 758497,
            "attributeType": "LongString",
            "attributeName": "AIDescription",
            "attributeRelationType": "None",
            "Value": "The PlayStation Portable (PSP) was Sony's foray into the handheld gaming market, released in Japan in 2004 and globally soon after. As the first handheld console to utilize an optical disc (UMD) format, it offered a significant leap in power and multimedia capabilities compared to its competitors, notably the Nintendo DS. The PSP boasted a vibrant 4.3-inch LCD screen and a sleek design, connecting to other devices and the internet via Wi-Fi. \n\nPopular titles like *Grand Theft Auto: Liberty City Stories*, *God of War: Ghost of Sparta*, and *Metal Gear Solid: Peace Walker* showcased its impressive graphics. While battery life and UMD loading times presented challenges, the PSP became a beloved portable entertainment device, selling over 80 million units. Later models included the Street and Go versions, and it was succeeded by the PlayStation Vita, retaining PSP game compatibility via the PlayStation Store until its closure in 2016."
          },
